Research in public administration has a developed concept for part of this problem: **administrative burden**. Pamela Herd and Donald Moynihan distinguish learning costs, the work required to discover that a programme exists and understand its rules; compliance costs, the time and effort spent on forms, documentation, deadlines and procedures; and psychological costs, including stress, stigma and other effects of citizen-state interaction. Their 2025 review emphasises that these frictions can limit access even for people legally entitled to services and may be hardest to overcome for those with the fewest resources and greatest needs. [Herd & Moynihan, *Journal of Economic Perspectives*, 2025](https://doi.org/10.1257/jep.20231394).

A dark comedy follows. A programme is designed for a person in crisis and access is tested with the question, “How well can you function while in crisis?” The person may be exhausted, unemployed, unfamiliar with the language and unable to understand the administrative system, so perhaps what is needed is seven offices, three portals, five passwords, twelve documents, a letter in which the simplest thought begins with *gemäß*, and a support line the person cannot use in the relevant language. Human-centred transformation successfully deployed. Green tick added.

Sarika Gupta’s experiment with a cash-transfer programme for disadvantaged women in Delhi shows the distinction between **formal entitlement** and **effective access** with unusual clarity. Only about one third of eligible people were enrolled. Providing information alone increased applications mainly among literate participants, while basic and intensive application assistance increased average application rates by about 41% and 70% respectively and expanded access among more vulnerable women. [Gupta, *American Economic Journal: Economic Policy*, 2026](https://doi.org/10.1257/pol.20240212).

The women did not become more deserving, the programme did not become more generous, and eligibility did not change. The bridge between entitlement and realisation changed. That is why **“the information is on the website” is not the same as “the person has meaningful access”**, just as “we informed you” is not the same as “we helped you”. Sometimes an entire administrative civilisation lives between those two sentences.

## Not every digital transformation is humanisation

Digitalisation is easily sold as an automatic synonym for progress. The paper disappears, the portal opens, the QR code appears, the app launches, and the annual report already smells like the future. But if the old procedure required six unnecessary actions and the new procedure requires the same six actions with an electronic signature, OTP, CAPTCHA, app and a PDF that weighs 5.2 MB instead of the permitted 5, the system has not become human-centred. It has merely **digitised its own friction**.

So not every digital transformation is humanisation; sometimes it is simply bureaucratisation with an electronic signature. Digitalisation asks how to move a procedure into a digital environment. Humanisation should ask an earlier question: **why does the person need to perform this step at all?** If the state already has the data, why enter it again; if entitlement can be established from existing registers, why require another application; if one contact could resolve the matter, why require three portals; if the digital channel does not work for a particular person, why should the alternative route become a punishment for insufficient digital fluency?

The OECD report *Modernising Access to Social Protection* notes that complex rules, information gaps and cumbersome procedures can produce non-take-up even when programmes are well designed and funded. It discusses linked administrative data and automatic enrolment as promising approaches while also emphasising legal, privacy and discrimination risks. A crucial point is that simply moving services online is not sufficient. [OECD, 2024](https://doi.org/10.1787/af31746d-en).

Sometimes the most advanced digital form is **no form at all**. A digital state that requires citizens to become increasingly competent operators of the digital state has not necessarily become more modern; it may simply have hired the citizen, unpaid, as a clerk in its own bureaucracy. In the old system a person could lose a piece of paper. In the new one the system can still lose the person, only with much better technology.

## A gate can test the wrong property

This may be the central methodological bug. If a fund wants to identify a strong artist but its application requires sophisticated grant-writing competence, the fund partly tests grant-writing competence. If a company wants a promising junior but requires two years of experience, it partly tests whether someone else has already taken the risk of giving that person a first chance. If a social programme wants to reach people in need but the procedure is extremely difficult, it partly tests administrative competence under conditions of scarcity.

The ability to pass a gate is therefore **not identical to the property for which the gate was created**. This is not an argument against gates as such. A bank genuinely needs to assess risk, an employer competence, a fund implementation capacity, a state legal eligibility. The more precise questions are: what exactly does this criterion measure, which secondary properties does it reward, and who is systematically filtered out before the primary property is even tested?

Document 015 on Polytypic Thinking led MET[Ȧ]CADEMY to a related principle: prediction is not identity, and a type is not an essence. Here a social analogue appears: **absence of previous access is not evidence of absence of future capacity**. The past is useful evidence, but it should not automatically acquire the right to close the future.

## The right to be not-yet-ready

Systems love ready-made people: the ready artist, ready employee, ready researcher, ready founder, ready citizen who already knows every form. But people often become ready **after they are allowed to be not-yet-ready inside a real task**. A student goes to work not because they are already an experienced professional but partly in order to become one. An early-career researcher receives a first project before having demonstrated the ability to run ten. An artist receives a first serious exhibition precisely because there could not have been a history of serious exhibitions before it. A founder looks for first capital before building the company that would no longer need first capital.

This sounds obvious until we notice how many institutions behave as though **adulthood should precede childhood**. Perhaps what is needed is a limited right to be unfinished within a safe first attempt, not a right to demand a positive outcome, but a right not to be disqualified merely because part of the required experience can only be produced **after entry**.

## The most expensive resource may not be money

Perhaps the most expensive resource is **the right to make an affordable mistake**. Not the right to act without consequences, but the possibility of trying at a scale where failure does not destroy a biography. For a well-resourced person, a failed start-up may become “experience”; for a person without reserves, one failed month may become debt or loss of housing. One person can fail five times and write a book about perseverance on the sixth attempt; another spends years after the first failure trying to return to the starting line.

“Take more risks” is an easy instruction when someone else pays the risk. A humanised system would ask not only whether the person is brave enough, but **whether failure has been made affordable enough for the person to try**. This is not sentimentality; it is risk design. Innovation without the possibility of controlled failure quickly becomes a privilege reserved for people who can afford several unsuccessful lives inside one lifetime.

## A first chance does not mean a grant for everyone

This is where a straw man can be built and heroically defeated. The right to a first chance does not mean every artist receives a grant, every applicant a job, every hypothesis a large budget or every risk public financing. **The right to a chance is not the right to a positive outcome.** Humanisation does not abolish selection; it changes the question of scale and function.

Perhaps the system needs a **proportionate way to test potential** without demanding proof of already-realised potential. Not immediately a large grant, but a small grant with a clear risk boundary; not a permanent contract, but a paid trainee path with genuine learning; not a major research budget, but an exploratory grant; not “we have declared you a genius”, but the more honest “we do not know yet, here is a safe way to find out”. Such a system does not promise infallibility. It recognises that uncertainty cannot be completely removed without removing the new along with it.

Some research funders already experiment with this kind of institutional humility. The Health Research Council of New Zealand’s Explorer Grants use anonymous assessment and, once proposals meet the required criteria, random ordering for funding until the available budget is exhausted: all qualifying applications are treated as equally eligible. [HRC New Zealand, Explorer Grants](https://gateway.hrc.govt.nz/funding/ideas-funding/2026-explorer-grants). This does not prove that lotteries are superior everywhere. It demonstrates another kind of methodological honesty: after a threshold, a system can sometimes admit that **it does not know enough to turn tiny score differences into a metaphysics of merit**.

## Perhaps we need a fair entrance more than a perfect selection system

What might a humanised entry point look like? Perhaps arts funding needs dedicated first-entry grants where the portfolio and a small testable proposal matter more than grant genealogy. Perhaps employment needs paid trainee and apprenticeship routes in which employers explicitly accept that some competence must be grown rather than purchased ready-made. Perhaps research needs first-funding mechanisms, blind elements, quality thresholds or partial randomisation where fine-grained rankings exceed real evaluative certainty. Perhaps social protection should use automatic enrolment when the state already has adequate data and provide real navigation for complex cases rather than links that merely explain how to navigate. Perhaps digital transformation should measure not the percentage of procedures placed online but the proportion of legitimate human goals that became meaningfully easier to achieve.

These mechanisms are not universal. Systems have different risks, scales and legal constraints. A shared principle may still be possible: **if a system exists to help a person move from A to B, it should not quietly require evidence that the person already lives in B**. And if a gate is necessary, perhaps the system should be able to explain what property it tests, why that property matters, what the cost of error is, and who is filtered out as a side effect.

## Measure not what the system offered, but what happened to the person

Institutions naturally count their own activity: programmes opened, money allocated, guidance pages published, consultations delivered, applications digitised. These are useful operational metrics, but they do not answer the central question. Humanisation shifts the focus from “what did the system offer?” to **“what actually happened to the person for whom the system exists?”**

